<%# The manual Microsoft 365 steps the app cannot do itself, on their own page.

    They lived inside the cost centre's Settings form as a collapsed section, where a
    wall of PowerShell and Graph JSON sat between the routine controls an operator
    edits weekly — the mailboxes, the notification address, the nominal codes. These
    steps are needed ONCE per cost centre, by somebody with Exchange Online or
    SharePoint admin rights, who is usually not the person editing the settings. %>
<%= render "shared/back_link", text: "Back to #{@cost_centre.name}",
           path: edit_admin_reimbursements_setting_path(@cost_centre.key) %>

<p class="mb-4 text-gray-600">
  What IT has to do in Microsoft 365 before this cost centre can send email, receive claims by
  email or back receipts up to SharePoint. You only need this when <strong>first setting the
  cost centre up</strong>; day-to-day use needs none of it. This page is safe to forward.
</p>

<% app_id = "b874d491-4edf-4b76-839d-84e534c7f7c0" %>
<% grant_mailboxes = [ @cost_centre.receive_mailbox, @cost_centre.send_mailbox ].map(&:presence).compact.uniq %>
<% code_class = "mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded bg-slate-800 p-3 text-xs text-slate-100" %>
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-sm text-gray-700">
    <p class="font-medium">
      These steps need Microsoft 365 admin permissions (Exchange Online / SharePoint admin).
      If that's not you, send this page to IT.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Entra app (<code>BlackLightning Reimbursements Mailbox</code>, App ID
      <code><%= app_id %></code>) already exists, so what's left is authorising this cost centre's
      own mailboxes and site below.
    </p>

    <div>
      <p class="font-medium">Authorise this cost centre's mailbox<%= "es" unless @cost_centre.receive_mailbox == @cost_centre.send_mailbox %></p>
      <p>
        Setting a mailbox on the settings page is <strong>not enough</strong>. The app holds no tenant-wide mail permission
        at all: Exchange hands it one mailbox at a time through a <strong>management scope</strong> listing
        the addresses it may touch (RBAC for Applications). Until
        <%= @cost_centre.receive_mailbox == @cost_centre.send_mailbox ? "it's" : "they're" %> in that scope,
        every Graph call (poll / draft / send) fails with <strong>403 AccessDenied</strong> and this cost
        centre's email-in and batch drafting silently break.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        <strong>The scope filter is replaced, not added to</strong>, so you have to pass every mailbox the
        app uses, not just the new one. Leave one out and you cut off a cost centre that was working.
        Don't type that list from memory, because a send mailbox is a separate field from a receive one and
        they usually differ. Ask the app:
      </p>
      <pre class="<%= code_class %>"><code>kamal app exec -i 'bin/rails graph:mailboxes'</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-2">
        That prints the <code>$env:</code> lines ready to paste. Then run the script from a checkout of this
        repo (<code>docs/graph-mailbox-rbac.ps1</code>), which is idempotent, so re-running it is safe:
      </p>
      <pre class="<%= code_class %>"><code># Create each shared mailbox first (M365 admin center &rarr; Teams &amp; groups &rarr; Shared
# mailboxes), then:
Connect-ExchangeOnline

$env:BL_GRAPH_APP_ID       = "<%= app_id %>"
$env:BL_GRAPH_SP_OBJECT_ID = "&lt;Object ID, from Entra &gt; ENTERPRISE APPLICATIONS&gt;"
# ...then paste the two $env: lines from `bin/rails graph:mailboxes`

./docs/graph-mailbox-rbac.ps1 -WhatIf   # read the filter it would write
./docs/graph-mailbox-rbac.ps1</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Read the <code>-WhatIf</code> filter before running it for real and check every mailbox is in it.
        Then confirm this cost centre came out in scope:
      </p>
      <% grant_mailboxes.each do |mailbox| %>
        <pre class="<%= code_class %>"><code>Test-ServicePrincipalAuthorization -Identity <%= app_id %> `
  -Resource <%= mailbox %> | Format-Table RoleName,AllowedResourceScope,InScope</code></pre>
      <% end %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500">
        Look for <code>InScope True</code> against <code>Application Mail Full Access</code>. That cmdlet
        bypasses the permission cache so it answers immediately, but the app doesn't: allow
        <strong>30 minutes to 2 hours</strong> before Run access check goes green. A red mailbox row inside
        that window isn't a second problem, so don't re-run the script at it.
        <strong>Address already in use?</strong> Find what holds the SMTP proxy with
        <code>Get-Recipient -Filter "EmailAddresses -eq 'smtp:THE-ADDRESS'"</code>, then free it or pick another.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="font-medium">Grant this app write access to the SharePoint site</p>
      <p>
        The app uses least-privilege <code>Sites.Selected</code>: it reaches only the sites you grant it.
        After setting the <strong>SharePoint site URL</strong> on the settings page, grant this app <code>write</code> on
        that site (once per site). The easiest way is <strong>Graph Explorer</strong>
        (<a class="underline" href="https://aka.ms/ge">aka.ms/ge</a>, a web tool), signed in as a SharePoint
        admin. These are Microsoft Graph calls, <strong>not PowerShell</strong>:
      </p>
      <% site_path = @cost_centre.sharepoint_graph_site_path %>
      <pre class="<%= code_class %>"><code># 1. Get the site id:
GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/<%= site_path || "TENANT.sharepoint.com:/sites/SiteName" %>
#    -&gt; copy "id" from the response

# 2. Grant THIS app write on THAT site only:
POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/SITE-ID/permissions
{
  "roles": ["write"],
  "grantedToIdentities": [
{ "application": { "id": "<%= app_id %>",
                   "displayName": "BlackLightning Reimbursements Mailbox" } }
  ]
}</code></pre>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
        The <code>POST</code> needs the <code>Sites.FullControl.All</code> delegated permission for your
        session (not for the app). If Graph Explorer says permissions are missing, open its
        <strong>Modify permissions</strong> tab, consent to <code>Sites.FullControl.All</code> as an admin,
        then re-run. Prefer PowerShell? Run the same with
        <code>Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "Sites.FullControl.All"</code> then <code>Invoke-MgGraphRequest</code>,
        but in a <strong>fresh window</strong>: the Exchange Online module from the mailbox step clashes with
        Microsoft.Graph in one session (the <code>Method not found: WithLogging</code> error). List or revoke
        later against the same <code>/sites/&lt;id&gt;/permissions</code> endpoint. Without the grant, uploads
        and the folder picker for this site fail with <strong>403</strong>. Don't fall back to tenant-wide
        <code>Sites.ReadWrite.All</code> / <code>Files.ReadWrite.All</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="font-medium">Graph permissions on the Entra app</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Mail: no Entra permission at all.</strong> Poll / draft / send come from the Exchange role
          assignment above (<code>Application Mail Full Access</code>), scoped to the mailbox list.
          Consenting <code>Mail.ReadWrite</code> or <code>Mail.Send</code> here would give the app every
          mailbox in the tenant and make the scoping decorative, because Exchange and Entra permissions are
          a union. Don't.
        </li>
        <li><code>Sites.Selected</code>: SharePoint, granted <code>write</code> per-site (above), not tenant-wide.</li>
        <li>
          <code>Sites.ReadWrite.All</code> and <code>Files.ReadWrite.All</code> are still consented
          tenant-wide, which is why SharePoint works today even for a site with no per-site grant. Retiring
          them is the open item in <code>docs/graph-mailbox-rbac.md</code>: grant the site first, confirm a
          batch upload, then revoke.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

<%= render CardComponent.new(title: "Then check it worked", html_class: "mt-4") do %>
  <p class="mb-0 text-sm text-gray-600">
    Back on
    <%= link_to "#{@cost_centre.name}'s settings",
                edit_admin_reimbursements_setting_path(@cost_centre.key), class: "underline" %>,
    <strong>Run access check</strong> probes this cost centre's mailboxes and site with the app's
    own credentials. Allow 30 minutes to 2 hours after the mailbox grant before it goes green:
    a red row inside that window is not a second problem.
  </p>
<% end %>
